Title: Tan Brick House
Credit: Written By
Author: Justin Del Vecchio
Draft date: June 2026

INT. COFFEE SHOP - MORNING

MIKA BOYKO, early 60s, Ukrainian accent, waits in line.

The customer ahead of him stares at his phone.

The cashier extends a handful of change.

Waits.

And waits.

MIKA
Ahem

CUSTOMER
Oh hey, thanks! Thanks!

CUSTOMER Collects change and moves along.  

Mika places two dollars, one quarter, and two pennies on counter.

MIKA
Hi, ususal.

MIKA takes coffee, finds table and sits with two friends.  

Older, clearly retirees.

Both are African American.

FRIEND 1
Hey Mika, how are you doing.

MIKA
OK.  Got up early, wathced sun rise, and walked here.  Traffic ridiculous today.  Crazy.  People speeed like mad right by school.

FRIEND 2
Hey, how is that house you gotta sell.

MIKA
I knew you would bring it up.  Knew it.  Was three months, right?

FRIEND 1 smiles

FRIEND 1
Seems longer than that!

Mikas hands cover and rub face.

MIKA
Is not.  Is just three months.  Since that St. Patrick's Day nonsense.  And it has not. It has not!

FRIEND 3 at table across peers over paper he reads.

FRIEND 3
What is the house.  What's that? 

FRIEND 2
Oh come on man.  Do you even listen to what we talk about?  The house. The house!

FRIEND 3
I've been in Florida for three months!

Mika holds up both hands as if to say all is ok.

MIKA
I will explain.  The house. What is the house? OK. The house is my wife's parents.  Wonderful people.  You hear stories about mouther-in-law and trouble.  She would do anything for you - no trouble at all. Quiet, beautiful.

Mika sips coffee.

MIKA
She would do anything for us.  Her husband, he died back in the 90s.  Smoked like you would not believe.  He was nice too, mind you.  Just the smoking.  The mother, she died in February.

FRIEND 3 
Sorry about that.

MIKA
Yeah, yeah.  Thanks.  So ... we get the house.  Nona has one sister. Stella. She lives in Austinn and she tells Nona 'You sell the house.  Keep the money.  I lived in Austin for last 22 years - you took care of mama all that time.  You should keep it."  The sister, she got some money from the mother and did not, mind you, need to clean out all the shit from house.  That was us for four weeks.

FRIEND 2
You got a house my friend!  A house!  House party.  Easy money.

Friend 3 says in passing while going back to reading the paper.

FRIEND 3
Austin ...

MIKA
Yeah, easy money.  And Austin is OK place.  Visited my sister-in-law there four or five times.  Visted twice in July.  Crazy. Hot, hot, hot.  Cannot leave house in daytime, could only leave at night.  Reminded me of Egypt where people in the summer only go outside at night.

Mika pauses for more coffee.

MIKA
Coffee is weak today.  Who make this!  Ok, so, that is all good.  All fine.  Sister OK with everything.  Wife OK with everything.  All we need to do is sell house, get money, move on.

FLASHBACK:

INT. MIKA'S KITCHEN - MORNING

Kitchen of an older tudor home.  

MIKA is inside with his wife NONA.  

Nona is impeccabily dressed and is beautiful.  

Both sit at the kitchen table.  

Nona eats  a sausage McMuffin.

MIKA
All we have to do is sell the house.  Put it on the market.  Set a reasonable price, I know what that price is, and we are good.

Nona says somewhat irritated.

NONA
I know.  I know! 

Nona then contemplates while holding the McMuffin.

NONA
We can sell the house and then, we can think about a condo in Ft. Lauderdale.

MIKA
I keep telling you - there is no way we can get a anything good in Ft. Lauderdal for the price we can get for this house no way.  Its not like this house equivalent to an ocean front in Ft. Lauderdale.

Nona finishes the McMuffin.

NONA
You keep saying that.  But you are not looking hard enough. I see these shows.  I see the Beachfront Bargins, they find these things all the time.  I will find something, let me look.

MIKA
You can look all you want.  All you want.  I will not stop you from doing that.  Dowload the app, search Ft. Lauderdale.  There is nothing to had for $200 thousand that gets what I know you want.  Not even close.  Its just ... its not even realistic Nona!

NONA
Exactly, because you do not let me look.  You are not looking right.

Mika shakes head, exhales, and looks away.

MIKA
Let's just sell this thing first.  Please.  Let's do that.  House is empty now.  Can we finally sell it.  Then we can argue about Ft. Lauderdale.

NONA
Yes, let's talk about the house.  I know exactly what we need to do.

MIKA
Good, good.  Consensus.  I know too.

NONA
Really, you know what to do.  You have read my mind and know.  Really.

MIKA
Yes, I have.  I have looked at the prices. Houses, comparables.

Nona shaking head

NONA
Comparables.  

MIKA
Yes.  Yes.  We should put a good price on the house.  Go 240K or 245K to start and get offers.  Move it out.

NONA
Really.  That is what we should do.

MIKA
Yes.  Really.

Nona smiles confidently.

NONA
This is what we are going to do.

Mika senses what is about to happen.

MIKA
No ... no.

NONA
We are going to use Mikey Drogai.

MIKA
No, no. Why. No. Why? Why Mikey?

NONA
Beacuse, he is a real estate agent.  He knows how to sell a house.  And he knows how to get the best price. And we know his family.

Mika's english is good but at times, gramatically incorrect when agitatted.  There is certainly a slavic accent though not that strong.

MIKA
